    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm reviewing a commercial lease agreement for a retail tenant here in Singapore. Could you explain the standard implications of a \"force majeure\" clause, specifically regarding how it might apply to a sudden and prolonged period of business interruption that prevents the tenant from operating?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In Singapore commercial leases, a force majeure clause typically exempts the tenant from liabilities like outstanding rent or break leases only if the event is beyond human control, and such clauses often contain specific exclusions for business interruptions arising from Government orders or public health measures. If a prolonged disruption occurs even under force majeure, the tenant may still be expected to pay reduced rent (not full outgoings) or face termination based on the strict time frames and definitions set out in the agreement. Since Singapore courts interpret these clauses narrowly to ensure lease certainty, it is crucial to review whether the PPA (Perpetual Past Agreement) or the specific Governing Law Act provisions classify the disruption as an automated business interruption or a true external event. You should also verify if the lease includes a force majeure extension timeline, as failure to secure a reasonable time to resume operations could void the exemption. It is always recommended to consult with a local legal professional to tailor the interpretation to the specific wording of your lease document.",
      "source": "target",
