    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. One follow up—the client is worried that if the disruption isn't explicitly named in the clause (like a pandemic or war), the landlord might argue it's just a standard business risk rather than a force majeure event. How do courts typically handle these \"unforeseen but unnamed\" events when determining if the clause is triggered?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Singapore courts typically apply a literal or 'common language meaning' interpretation to force majeure clauses, requiring the specific event described to match the circumstances of the disruption. Even if an event like a pandemic isn't explicitly named, it may still be covered if it is unforeseeable, unforeseen, or inherently extraordinary, but the definition of the event in the clause was too narrow to encompass the specific circumstances. Because the law generally rejects broad legislative or contractual intent, ambiguity often benefits the landlord, meaning unnamed events must be proven as falling squarely within the \"undefined\" event category. Therefore, relying solely on the literal interpretation suggests the risk, as courts rarely extend force majeure protections to cover standard business risks. It is advisable to add a catch-all definition or specific event triggers to your lease to prevent such narrow interpretation issues.",
      "source": "target",
