      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. We're also looking at potential \"frustration of contract\" claims as an alternative if the force majeure clause falls short. In a retail context, what kind of threshold or \"degree of disruption\" would usually be required for a court to consider a contract frustrated rather than just being commercially difficult for the tenant?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In Singapore, the threshold for \"frustration of contract\" is exceptionally high, requiring the supervening event to be so radical that it renders the contract's fundamental purpose futile. For a retail lease, this usually demands a disruption that strips the tenant of the core benefit of the business, rather than just making it less profitable or commercially unviable. Courts will likely reject frustration arguments if the tenant can still legally operate, even if the cost of doing so outweighs potential revenues. Consequently, mere commercial risks or significant but not catastrophic interruptions rarely meet this stringent legal standard. Given these strict requirements, relying on frustration is often a last resort when no other contractual mechanism, such as force majeure, applies.",
